## Workspace pattern

### Multi-Root Workspace
The pattern in which an agent session attaches several physical directories at
once — shared synced roots plus machine-local roots — instead of assembling them
under one combined folder. Replaced the retired symlink-assembled root, whose
linked contents harness file viewers could not display.

### Shared Root
A workspace root every teammate sees. When it lives on synced storage rather than
in git, sync is both the versioning and the distribution channel: there is no
review step and no diff, so any file written under it reaches every teammate's
mount silently. Member-specific harness configuration must never be written inside
one.

### Primary Folder
The single folder an agent harness treats as its project scope for a session. The
harness persists session-local state — permission grants, local settings overrides
— beneath it, and recreates that state on later sessions. The primary folder must
be machine-local, never a shared synced root.

### Launch Shape
How a session is started: which folder is the Primary Folder and which additional
roots are attached, persistently in user scope or per-session. The durable defense
against leaking personal harness state into a shared root lives at launch-shape
level, because detection tooling can only warn after a bad shape has already
written files.

### Workspace Descriptor
The machine-local declaration of where a member's workspace roots live. Every
machine-local root is declared explicitly by the member — never inferred from
sibling directories or directory shape — and the descriptor stays on that machine;
it is never synced, shared, or committed.

Tools that need a machine-local root resolve it from the declaration and fail
closed when it is absent, stale, or invalid: a missing declaration stops the
operation instead of falling back to a guess. Regeneration must carry forward the
full set of previously declared roots — a re-run cannot silently narrow the
declaration. Lanes inside the shared workspace tree are the scoped exception: they
are physical siblings of the support tree and may be derived from its location.

### Binding Token
A named placeholder that shared documents use to reference a machine-local root
(`${AGENTIC_REPO_CHECKOUT_ROOT}`), resolved on each reader's machine through the
Workspace Descriptor. Binding tokens replace sibling-relative paths, which stopped
resolving once workspace roots ceased to share a common parent.

### Workspace Doctor
The diagnostic that reports whether a member's workspace matches the declared
multi-root pattern — support tree resolvable, machine-local roots validly declared,
no member-specific harness configuration inside a shared root — and which
capability tiers the session has. It warns on recoverable violations (a harness can
recreate them) and fails only when the workspace cannot be resolved or its
declaration is invalid.

### Capability Tier
A band of things a session can do, gated on what is actually present: shared
context (no git needed), repo questions from digests (no git needed), checkout
inspection (git plus hydrated checkouts), checkout refresh, and any credentialed
external access. The doctor reports each tier as available or locked, with the one
concrete step that unlocks it.

### Repo Digest
A generated per-repository summary — purpose, top-level layout, last-refreshed
commit, freshness dates — stored in shared context so repository questions can be
answered with no git installed and no checkout open. Orientation only; deeper
questions drop to the checkout.

